WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.870
How detergents clean, your raw materials, and staying safe.

2
00:00:04.220 --> 00:00:09.000
This is the video for module one of Detergent and Cleaning Products Production.

3
00:00:10.250 --> 00:00:20.190
Every cleaning product depends on one kind of ingredient, a surfactant. That word is short for surface active agent. One end, the head, loves water.

4
00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:24.980
The other end, the tail, loves oil and grease, and hates water.

5
00:00:26.230 --> 00:00:33.360
When you wash a greasy pot, the tails push into the grease, and thousands of molecules surround each small drop of oil.

6
00:00:34.610 --> 00:00:45.250
Now the grease has a coat that water can hold, so rinsing carries it away. That is why plain water cannot remove palm oil from a plate, but water with detergent can.

7
00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:59.050
The keg liquid soap sold in Nigeria is almost always a synthetic detergent, not true soap. Multipurpose liquid soap is a good first product, because everyone buys it again and again.

8
00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:03.580
Liquid hand wash comes second, once the first is right every time.

9
00:01:04.830 --> 00:01:11.630
Do not manufacture bleach or disinfectants as a beginner. Germ-killing claims need proof and approval.

10
00:01:11.980 --> 00:01:17.340
A detergent cleans by lifting dirt and grease. Never label it as a disinfectant.

11
00:01:18.590 --> 00:01:30.850
Sulphonic acid is a thick, dark brown, oily liquid. As bought, it is a strong, corrosive acid. It only becomes a cleaning agent after it is neutralised with an alkali.

12
00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:47.310
SLES, often sold as texapon, is gentler on skin and foams well. A label saying 70% means 100 g of paste holds about 70 g of real surfactant. Buy on known strength, not just price.

13
00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:56.690
Caustic soda is a strong alkali used to neutralise the acid. Soda ash is milder, and fizzes when it meets acid.

14
00:01:57.940 --> 00:02:03.960
STPP is a builder. It ties up the minerals in hard water so the surfactant works better.

15
00:02:05.210 --> 00:02:12.100
Nitrosol, CMC and salt give the product body. Remember, more foam does not mean better cleaning.

16
00:02:13.350 --> 00:02:25.360
Many local recipes use formalin as a preservative. Formaldehyde is classed as a human carcinogen, so avoid it. Use a detergent preservative at the dose on its data sheet.

17
00:02:26.610 --> 00:02:44.170
Sulphonic acid and caustic soda can burn skin and damage eyes within seconds. So work with a routine. Splash goggles that seal, gloves over the wrists, long sleeves, an apron and closed shoes. Mix outdoors or with open windows, never in your kitchen.

18
00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:55.720
Measure cold water first, in plastic or stainless steel. Never aluminium. Caustic soda attacks it and gives off hydrogen gas, which can catch fire.

19
00:02:56.970 --> 00:03:04.740
Add the caustic soda slowly to the water, stirring. Never pour water onto caustic soda. It can boil up and splash.

20
00:03:05.990 --> 00:03:10.080
The solution gets very hot. Let it cool before adding it to anything.

21
00:03:11.330 --> 00:03:16.220
Pour sulphonic acid slowly into the water or batch, never the other way round.

22
00:03:17.470 --> 00:03:22.840
Neutralising gives off heat, so add the alkali in small portions and stir between them.

23
00:03:24.090 --> 00:03:32.010
Household bleach mixed with acids, like many toilet cleaners, vinegar or sulphonic acid, releases chlorine gas.

24
00:03:33.260 --> 00:03:41.770
Mixed with ammonia, found in some glass cleaners and urine, it releases chloramine gases. Both can damage the lungs and kill.

25
00:03:43.020 --> 00:03:48.590
So never add bleach to any product you make, or put it in a container that once held acid.

26
00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:54.720
Tell customers clearly. Use bleach on its own, rinse, then use other cleaners.

27
00:03:55.970 --> 00:04:07.620
Keep a large container of clean water within reach. A splash in the eye needs rinsing with running water for at least 15 minutes, holding the eyelid open. Then go to a hospital.

28
00:04:08.870 --> 00:04:17.940
On skin, remove soaked clothing and rinse for at least 15 minutes. Do not use vinegar or soda on a burn. Water is the right first aid.

29
00:04:19.190 --> 00:04:32.740
If someone breathes fumes, leave for fresh air at once. If a chemical is swallowed, do not make the person vomit. Rinse the mouth and go to hospital with the container. And never store chemicals in drink bottles.

30
00:04:33.990 --> 00:04:43.270
So remember. Surfactants let water carry grease away. Start with multipurpose liquid soap. Buy labelled chemicals and avoid formalin.

31
00:04:43.620 --> 00:04:48.480
Add caustic and acid to water, slowly. And never mix bleach with other cleaners.

32
00:04:49.730 --> 00:05:00.460
Your practice: survey five cleaning products near you, build your raw materials sheet, set up your safety station, and put a safety card on the wall with the hospital number.

33
00:05:00.810 --> 00:05:02.960
Now continue with the lessons below.
